### `WT004` WT004_reinitialisation/registry.rs:37

`CreateProfile.profile` uses `init_if_needed` on program state, and no handler checks whether the account already holds data. A caller can pass an existing account and have the handler overwrite state that belongs to someone else.

```rust
    pub profile: Account<'info, Profile>,
```

**Fix.** Ask whether the account is already initialised before writing the fields that establish ownership — compare the stored authority against `Pubkey::default()`, or require it to match the caller. Where creation and update are genuinely different operations, give them separate instructions and use plain `init`.

<sub>high · confidence medium · `CreateProfile.profile` · id `410ab9cbe9e9fbf2`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/reinitialization-attacks) · [reference](https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-constraints)</sub>

### `WT005` WT005_missing_has_one/treasury.rs:33

`Treasury` stores `authority: Pubkey`, and `Withdraw` takes both `treasury` and `authority`, but never checks that they match. The stored key records a relationship the instruction does not enforce, so any `treasury` can be used with any `authority`.

```rust
    pub treasury: Account<'info, Treasury>,
```

**Fix.** Add `has_one = <field>` to the account that stores the key, or assert it explicitly with `constraint = account.field == other.key()`. Deriving the account's address from the key with `seeds` enforces the same relationship and is stronger still.

<sub>high · confidence medium · `Withdraw.treasury` · id `0dc13ff4575e2169`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/account-data-matching) · [reference](https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-constraints)</sub>

### `WT006` WT006_non_canonical_bump/market.rs:38

`OpenMarket.market` derives its address with `bump = market_bump`, which comes from the instruction argument `market_bump`. The caller chooses the bump, so they can derive several valid addresses for the same seeds. Use a bare `bump` and let Anchor supply the canonical one.

```rust
        bump = market_bump,
```

**Fix.** Write `bump` on its own and let Anchor derive the canonical bump, storing `ctx.bumps.<account>` if later instructions need it. Where a bump is re-derived, read it from the account (`bump = account.bump`) rather than from the caller.

<sub>high · confidence high · `OpenMarket.market` · id `f8c3eb4d2afe9cab`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/bump-seed-canonicalization) · [reference](https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/pdas)</sub>

### `WT009` WT009_sysvar_spoofing/clocked.rs:43

`Claim.clock` is named for a sysvar but is an unchecked account with no address constraint. Sysvars live at fixed addresses and are otherwise ordinary accounts, so a caller can substitute one with contents of their choosing.

```rust
    pub clock: AccountInfo<'info>,
```

**Fix.** Use `Sysvar<'info, Clock>` and its siblings, which assert the address. For sysvars Anchor has no type for, pin it with `#[account(address = sysvar::instructions::ID)]`.

<sub>high · confidence high · `Claim.clock` · id `54d2cd9bdf500a57`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/sysvar-spoofing) · [reference](https://docs.rs/anchor-lang/latest/anchor_lang/accounts/sysvar/struct.Sysvar.html)</sub>

### `WT010` WT010_unchecked_deserialisation/config_store.rs:21

`read_config` calls `try_deserialize_unchecked`, which does not verify the account discriminator. Any account owned by this program can then be read as this type, regardless of what it actually is.

```rust
    pub fn read_config(ctx: Context<ReadConfig>) -> Result<()> {
```

**Fix.** Use `try_deserialize`, which verifies the discriminator, or the typed `Account<'info, T>` which does it before the handler runs. The `_unchecked` variants are for accounts you have just created and know the layout of.

<sub>high · confidence high · `config_store::read_config` · id `1e42fc9635a63add`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-types) · [reference](https://github.com/coral-xyz/sealevel-attacks)</sub>

## Medium

### `WT008` WT008_unsafe_close/closer.rs:23

`close_position` sets an account's lamports to zero without clearing its data. The runtime only reclaims the account at the end of the transaction, so a caller can send lamports back within the same transaction and keep the account, its data intact.

```rust
    pub fn close_position(ctx: Context<ClosePosition>) -> Result<()> {
```

**Fix.** Prefer Anchor's `close = destination` constraint, which drains the lamports, zeroes the data, and assigns the account to the system program. Closing by hand requires all three — zeroing the balance alone leaves the account revivable within the same transaction.

<sub>medium · confidence medium · `closer::close_position` · id `8af310d24a646694`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/closing-accounts) · [reference](https://www.anchor-lang.com/docs/account-constraints)</sub>

### `WT011` WT011_duplicate_mutable/transfer.rs:34

`Transfer` takes 2 mutable `Balance` accounts (from, to) with nothing asserting they differ. A caller can pass the same address for all of them; Anchor deserialises it once per field, and only the last write survives.

```rust
    pub from: Account<'info, Balance>,
```

**Fix.** Assert that the accounts differ: `constraint = first.key() != second.key()`. Anchor cannot infer it, because passing the same account twice is legitimate in other instructions.

<sub>medium · confidence medium · `Transfer.from` · id `ed7f4c41ecffac78`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/developers/courses/program-security/duplicate-mutable-accounts) · [reference](https://github.com/coral-xyz/sealevel-attacks)</sub>

## Low

### `WT012` WT012_alloc_in_loop/rebalance.rs:23

`pool.weights.clone()` in `rebalance` allocates on every iteration. Hoist it out of the loop, or borrow instead — compute units are a hard per-transaction budget.

```rust
            let snapshot = pool.weights.clone();
```

**Fix.** Hoist the allocation out of the loop, or borrow instead of cloning. Where the collection is only read, iterate over a reference.

<sub>low · confidence medium · `rebalance::rebalance` · id `61d128c53432a529`</sub>

<sub>[reference](https://solana.com/docs/core/fees#compute-budget)</sub>
